{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus outcomes across the whole 30‑day horizon (often by ~2–10 €/t), especially during downtrends.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: the model follows the direction but declines too slowly; apply a stronger negative slope when the last 1–2 weeks show falling prices.",
    "False rebound/mean‑reversion overshoot: after sharp drops, the model predicts rebounds back toward mid‑70s/80s that do not materialize; dampen mean‑reversion strength after downside breaks.",
    "Horizon effect: error grows with lead time—near-term (days 1–5) is mildly high, mid/long horizon (days 10–30) becomes materially high; use an increasing downward correction with horizon.",
    "Regime shift miss: when prices break below ~70, the model keeps forecasting a return above ~72–78; introduce a level-dependent adjustment (if spot <70, cap forecasts and shift the whole path downward).",
    "Volatility asymmetry: model underestimates depth of selloffs (predicts higher lows); apply extra negative adjustment proportional to recent downside volatility (e.g., large negative returns in last 5–10 days)."
  ]
}
